<%#
  Privacy Policy, in the 2009 shape: a letter-spaced title on the sky, then
  bold section headings with plain paragraphs and bullets. No panels, no cards.

  Everything a paragraph claims here has to be true of the build: guest
  browsing, one session cookie, hashed passwords, sign-in records, moderation
  records, and no payment details anywhere. Nothing is described as collected
  unless a table actually collects it.
%>
<%- include('../partials/head', { pageTitle: pageTitle, bodyClass: 'fb-privacy-page' }) %>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/legal2009.css?v=1">
<%- include('../partials/header') %>

<main class="fb-legal2009">
  <div class="fb-container">
    <h1 class="fb-legal2009__title">FieldBrik Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="fb-legal2009__note">
      Draft, pending legal review. This describes what FieldBrik collects and why, in the terms the
      site is actually built to. Last edited 7 November 2026.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">About FieldBrik</h2>
    <p>FieldBrik is an online social building site. FieldBrik works like this:</p>
    <ul class="fb-legal2009__list">
      <li>a user creates a character, which is their identity on FieldBrik: a name, a description and
        an avatar they colour themselves</li>
      <li>users interact through place pages, personal messaging kept on this site, the forum, friends
        lists and catalogs; there is no chat inside a game yet, because the client that would play a
        place does not exist</li>
      <li>users build places out of parts and publish them; a published place has its own page and
        appears in Games</li>
      <li>users search games, read the catalog and look at other members' pages, and a signed-out
        visitor can do all of that without an account</li>
      <li>users earn Tickets by taking part and may hold Bucks; both are recorded as ledger entries on
        the account</li>
      <li>users buy catalog items with either currency, and the item is added to their inventory</li>
      <li>nothing on FieldBrik is paid for: there is no checkout, and no card or bank details are
        collected anywhere on the site</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Requested Information</h2>
    <p>
      Visitors may browse as guests. A guest can read places, the catalog, the forum, news and members'
      pages, and stays anonymous while doing it: no name, no address, no message and no place of their
      own.
    </p>
    <p>
      An account is needed to save anything, publish a place, send a message or take part in the
      economy. Creating one asks for three things: a username, an email address and a password. The
      password is stored only as a one-way hash, so it cannot be read back by staff or by anybody who
      copies the database.
    </p>
    <p>
      The username is public: it is shown on a member's page, on the places and catalog items they
      made, and on the messages they send. The email address is never shown to other members. A name
      that would look like FieldBrik staff, or that is abusive, is refused by the server at sign-up,
      however the form is sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signing in is recorded: the time of the attempt, whether it worked, and the network address it
      came from. That record is what lets a session be ended and misuse investigated. Sessions are
      held in a cookie, described below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">What Is Not Collected</h2>
    <p>
      No payment details, no advertising or tracking network, no location, no contact list and no
      device data. There is no third-party analytics script on any page, and no profile of what a
      member reads is built to sell.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Why It Is Collected</h2>
    <p>
      To run the account, show the member's page, deliver messages, keep the economy honest and
      enforce the <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>. Moderation records exist so that a
      restriction cannot be escaped by making a new account.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      One cookie, and only one: the session cookie that keeps a member signed in. It is marked
      HttpOnly, so a script on the page cannot read it, and it stops working when the member signs out
      or the session ends.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Who Can See What</h2>
    <p>
      A username, avatar, description, badges and published places are visible to anyone. An email
      address is visible to nobody but the account it belongs to. Private messages are readable by the
      sender and the recipient, and by staff only when a message is reported or a safety investigation
      needs it. Reports are never shown to the member they are about.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Moderation Records</h2>
    <p>
      A warning, a restriction or a ban is recorded with its reason, when it was issued, when it ends
      and which staff member issued it. The account it applies to can see it. It is kept after the
      restriction lifts, because a pattern of behaviour is what moderation acts on.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">How Long It Is Kept</h2>
    <p>
      Account data is kept while the account exists. When an account is closed, profile content is
      removed and the identifying fields are cleared. Moderation and ledger records are retained in a
      form that no longer identifies the member, because deleting them on request would let a ban or a
      currency record be erased.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Parents</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/parents">Parents</a> page explains what an account involves, what is asked for at
      sign-up, and how to ask for an account to be closed.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Changes</h2>
    <p>
      When this policy changes, the date at the top of the page changes with it. A change that widens
      what is collected will be announced on the site before it takes effect.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fb-legal2009__head">Contact</h2>
    <p>Privacy requests go through the <a href="/support">support page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</main>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
</body>
</html>
